# STAGE-23A — IMPLEMENTATION CONSTITUTION

I would not call it another phase.

I would call it:

STAGE-23A — COREI Implementation Constitution

Reason:

Stage-23 defines

What COREI is.

Stage-23A defines

How COREI must always be built.

Very different purposes.

## Purpose

Convert constitutional architecture into constitutional engineering.

Every implementation decision must be validated against this constitution.

No exception.

## Mission

Ensure COREI remains institutionally consistent for decades regardless of:

engineering teams
technologies
frameworks
AI tools
vendors
developers
future expansion
This is not architecture.

This is engineering law.

Proposed Structure

I think Stage-23A should contain approximately 20 Engineering Laws.

Not recommendations.

Not guidelines.

Laws.

## LAW-01 — Constitution Before Code

Architecture defines implementation.

Implementation shall never redefine architecture.

## LAW-02 — Responsibility Before Technology

Every implementation begins by identifying the institutional responsibility.

Never begin with a framework.

## LAW-03 — Capability Before Component

Build Institutional Capabilities.

Components merely render capabilities.

## LAW-04 — Workspace Before Layout

A Workspace represents professional work.

Layouts merely visualize work.

## LAW-05 — Runtime Before Rendering

The Workbench Runtime owns behaviour.

Rendering owns presentation.

## LAW-06 — Contracts Before Implementation

Every subsystem requires a contract before implementation.

No contract.

No code.

## LAW-07 — Composition Before Duplication

New behaviour shall be achieved through composition.

Never duplicate.

## LAW-08 — Services Before Coupling

Subsystems communicate through services.

Never through direct dependencies.

## LAW-09 — Configuration Before Hardcoding

Institutional behaviour shall be configurable.

Hardcoded behaviour requires constitutional justification.

## LAW-10 — State Before UI

Institutional state exists independently of the interface.

UI observes state.

UI never owns state.

## LAW-11 — Context Before Action

Every action shall execute within explicit institutional context.

Never rely upon implicit assumptions.

## LAW-12 — Explainability Before Automation

Automation must explain itself.

Invisible reasoning is prohibited.

## LAW-13 — Governance Before Convenience

Convenience shall never compromise governance.

## LAW-14 — Observability Before Optimization

Measure first.

Optimize afterwards.

## LAW-15 — Evidence Before Change

Every improvement requires evidence.

Opinion alone is insufficient.

## LAW-16 — Human Authority Before AI

AI recommends.

Humans decide.

Unless constitutionally delegated.

## LAW-17 — Scalability Before Features

Every feature must pass the Anchor Law scalability gate.

Support:

Multi Account
Multi Broker
Multi Strategy
Multi Portfolio
Multi Asset
Multi Exchange
Multi Market
Multi Currency
Multi Region
Multi Team
Multi Role
Multi Monitor
Multi Custodian
Multi Jurisdiction
24×7 Operations

before implementation.

## LAW-18 — Evolution Before Perfection

No implementation is final.

Every implementation shall support governed evolution.

## LAW-19 — Institution Before Individual

Personal coding preferences never override institutional architecture.

## LAW-20 — COREI Before Technology

Technologies change.

COREI remains.

Architecture survives.

Frameworks do not.

