### My Final Recommendation

Looking back at everything we've designed—from the backend platform, through the Workbench, Capabilities, Workspaces, Cognitive Architecture, and Evolution Architecture—I would make one final organizational recommendation.

Separate "Architecture" from "Engineering"

Create two top-level documentation families:

docs/

├── constitution/
│   ├── COREI Anchor Law
│   ├── Master Trader Persona
│   ├── Stage-23 Institutional Operator Workstation
│   ├── Stage-23A Implementation Constitution
│   └── Institutional Meta Model
│
├── architecture/
│   ├── Workbench
│   ├── Capability Architecture
│   ├── Workspace Architecture
│   ├── Cognitive Architecture
│   ├── Evolution Architecture
│   └── Runtime Architecture
│
├── engineering/
│   ├── UI Implementation
│   ├── Runtime Implementation
│   ├── Capability Development Guide
│   ├── Workspace Development Guide
│   ├── Extension SDK
│   └── Engineering Standards
│
└── implementation/
    ├── Stage-24
    ├── Stage-25
    ├── Stage-26
    └── ...

This separation creates a clear hierarchy:

Constitution defines immutable institutional principles.
Architecture defines system structure and responsibilities.
Engineering defines how architecture is implemented.
Implementation contains the actual execution stages.

I believe this is the cleanest end state for Stage-23. It establishes a stable constitutional and architectural foundation that can guide years of implementation without requiring the team to repeatedly revisit first principles, while remaining fully aligned with the COREI Anchor Law, the Master Trader Persona, and your vision of an institutional-grade operating system.
